So you've been working on neural nets for a long time.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:16.890
I'd love to hear your personal story of how did you get started in AI?

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.210
How did you end up working with neural networks?

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.450
So I got interested in,

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:25.010
I was always interested in the intelligence in general,

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.210
like the armatures of intelligence in humans,

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.250
you know, that got me interested into, you know,

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:32.170
human evolution when I was a kid and, uh,

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:33.170
this is in France.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:36.250
It was in France, I was, you know, um,

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:39.050
you know, in middle school or something and, uh,

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:42.330
you know, I was interested in technology and like, you know, space, etc.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.090
You know, my favorite movie was 2001 Space Odyssey, you know,

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:48.250
you had intelligent machines, you know, space travel

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:50.410
and human evolution as kind of the themes,

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:52.490
that was what I was fascinated by.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:55.170
Um, and, and, you know, the concept of intelligent machine,

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.050
I think really kind of, uh, appealed to me.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:00.770
And then, um, you know, I studied electrical engineering

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:02.490
and when I was, uh, at school,

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.450
I was maybe in second year of engineering school,

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:07.170
I, um, stumbled on a book,

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:08.730
uh, which was actually a philosophy book.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:11.010
It was a debate between, uh,

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.890
Noam Chomsky, the computational linguist at MIT

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.930
and Jean Piaget, who is a cognitive psychologist,

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.690
you know, sort of, uh, psychology of,

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.130
of child development in, uh, in Switzerland.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:25.210
And it was basically a debate between, you know,

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.410
nature and nurture, where Chomsky,

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:28.810
arguing for the fact that, you know,

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:31.170
language is, is a lot of unique structure

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:32.290
and Piaget saying, you know,

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:34.330
a lot of it is learned and, etc.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:38.610
And on the side of Piaget was, uh, uh,

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.890
a, a, a transcription of, uh, a person,

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:43.930
you know, each of these guys sort of brought their teams

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:47.010
of, of, of people to, uh, to, you know,

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:48.610
argue for their side.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:52.650
And on the side of Piaget was Seymour Papert from MIT,

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.930
who had worked on the perceptual model,

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.250
uh, one of the first machines capable of running.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.650
And I never heard of the perceptual,

00:01:58.650 --> 00:01:59.810
and I read this article and say,

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.610
a machine capable of running, that sounds wonderful.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:06.170
And so I, I, I started going to, uh, uh,

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.930
several university libraries and, and searching for all,

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:11.090
you know, everything I could find that talked about

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:13.330
the perceptual and then realized there was a lot of papers

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.530
from the fifties, but, but it kind of stopped

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:17.770
at the end of the sixties with a book that was co-authored

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.010
by the same Seymour Papert.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.490
What year was this then?

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:24.530
So this was 1980, roughly.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.090
And so I, I, I specialized, uh,

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.250
I did a couple of projects with some of the math professor

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.130
in my school on, on kind of neural nets, essentially,

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:34.690
but there was no one I could talk to who, you know,

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:36.770
had worked on this because the field basically

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:38.690
had disappeared in the meantime, right?

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:40.610
This is 1980, nobody was working on this.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.930
And, uh, you know, experimented with this, uh,

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:48.050
a little bit, you know, writing kind of simulation software

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.930
of various kinds, uh, reading about neuroscience.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:56.050
Um, when I finished my, uh, engineering studies,

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:59.650
I studied chip design, you get a site design at the time.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.610
So it's something completely different.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:04.050
Uh, and when I finished, I, I really wanted to kind of

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.410
do research on this and I, you know,

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:07.610
figured out already that, uh, at the time that

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:09.690
the important question was how you train neural nets

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:10.810
with multiple layers.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:12.690
It was pretty clear in the literature of the sixties

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.890
that that was the important question that was,

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:16.810
that had been left unsolved.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.770
Uh, and the idea of hierarchy and everything,

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:23.090
you know, I'd read, uh, Fukushima's article

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:24.290
on the new cognitron, right?

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:26.130
Which was this sort of hierarchical architecture,

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.770
very similar to now what we now call conv- convolution nets.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:32.490
But, you know, um, with, uh, without really,

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.610
without a back prop style learning algorithms.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:39.130
Um, and I met a small, uh, I met people who were

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:43.850
in a small independent lab, uh, in France, uh, that, um,

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:46.770
they were interested in what, what they called at the time

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:47.690
automata networks.

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:51.410
Uh, and they gave me a couple of papers,

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.770
the people on Hopfield networks, which, you know,

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:56.010
is not very popular anymore, but it's, uh, you know,

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.490
the first associative memories with, uh, with neural nets.

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:01.330
And that, that paper kind of revived the interest

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:03.730
of some research communities into neural nets

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:04.610
in the early eighties.

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:10.770
Uh, whereby, um, uh, mostly, uh, physicists and, um,

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:13.490
so condensed matter physicists and a few psychologists.

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:16.090
It was, it was still not okay for engineers

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.810
and computer scientists to talk about neural nets.

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:21.890
And they also showed me another paper, uh, that had just been

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:26.490
distributed as a preprint, uh, uh, whose title was

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:28.530
optimal perceptual inference.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:31.570
And this was the first paper on, on Boltzmann machines

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:33.290
by Jeff Hinton and Terry Sanorski.

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:34.930
It was talking about hidden units.

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:37.170
It was talking about basically the problem of learning,

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:42.290
you know, multilayer neural nets with, you know,

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:44.570
that are, you know, are more capable than just

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:45.490
linear classifiers.

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:50.170
Um, so I said, you know, I need to meet these people

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:53.210
because they, they're really interested in the right problem.

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:58.170
Um, and a couple of years later, I was, after I started my PhD,

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:02.610
um, I participated in a workshop in, uh, in Les Uches,

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:05.930
and that was organized by the people I was working with.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:09.930
And, uh, Terry was one of the, one of the speakers

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:10.490
at that workshop.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:11.770
So I met him at that time.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:13.250
It was like early eighties now.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:15.930
This is 1985, early 1985.

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:19.650
So I met Terry Sanorski in 1985 in the workshop in France in Les Uches.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:23.010
And, um, a lot of people were there, you know, from the sort of early neural net,

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:26.610
you know, jump up fields and a lot of people working on sort of theoretical

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:27.770
neuroscience and stuff like that.

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:29.730
It was a fascinating, uh, workshop.

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:34.090
I met also, uh, a couple of people from, a couple of people from Bell Labs

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:35.850
who eventually hired me at Bell Labs.

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:36.210
Okay.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.130
But this was several years before I finished my PhD.

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:42.890
So, um, I talked to Terry Sanorski and I was, uh, telling him about what I

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:45.250
was working on, which was some version of Backprop at the time.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:47.090
This was before Backprop was a paper.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:54.050
Um, and Terry was working on Netalk, uh, at the time.

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:58.370
Uh, this was, you know, before the, the, you know, the Rommel Hart,

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:01.210
Hinton Williams paper on Backprop had been published, but you know,

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:04.410
he was friends with Jeff, you know, this information was circulating.

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:07.610
So he was already working on trying to make this work for Netalk,

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:08.690
but he didn't, he didn't tell me.

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:14.570
Um, and, uh, he went back to the U S and told Jeff, there is some kid in France

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:16.010
who was working on the same stuff we were working on.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:16.650
Oh, I see.

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:20.970
And then a couple of months, a few months later in June, uh, there was, uh,

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:26.570
another conference in France that where Jeff was a keynote speaker and

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:28.210
he gave a talk on Boston machines.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:30.930
Of course he was working on, you know, the Backprop paper.

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:35.850
Um, and he gave this talk and then there is, you know, 50 people around him

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:36.810
who want to talk to him.

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:40.450
And the first thing he said to the organizer is, is, is, you know,

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:44.490
this guy, Jan LeCun and it's because he had read my paper in the proceedings

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:47.410
that was written in French and he could sort of read French and he could see

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:49.330
the math and he could figure out what sort of Backprop.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:52.690
And so, so we had lunch together and that's where we became friends.

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:57.690
So that's because you basically, you know, independently, multiple groups

00:06:57.690 --> 00:07:00.570
independently reinvented or invented Backprop pretty much.

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:00.970
Right.

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:05.890
Or realize that the whole idea of chain rule or, uh, what the optimal

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:08.730
control people call it the adjoint state method, which is, you know,

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:10.850
really the context in which Backprop was reinvented.

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:13.890
This is a context of optimal control by the early sixties.

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:17.570
This idea that you could use gradient descent basically with multiple

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:21.530
kinds of stages, um, is, uh, is what Backprop really is.

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:24.250
And that popped up in various contexts at various times.

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:31.570
Um, and, but I think, you know, the Rommel Hart Hinton-Wenjers

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:32.930
paper is the one that popularized it.

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:33.850
Yeah.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:39.810
And then, um, you know, fast forward a few years, uh, you wound up at AT&T

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:43.330
Bell Labs where you invented among many things, uh, the net, which we

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:45.250
talked about in the, in the, in the course.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:49.570
And I remember when way back, I was a summer intern at AT&T Bell Labs,

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:53.010
uh, work of Michael Kearns and a few others and hearing about your work even

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:58.290
back then, so tell me more about your, your AT&T, the net experience.

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:01.450
So what happened is actually started working on CompTrial Net when I was,

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:06.170
uh, uh, a postdoc at University of Toronto with Jeff Hinton.

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:08.010
Uh, I did the first experiment.

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:10.650
I wrote the code there and did the first experiments there that showed that

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:14.370
if you had a very small dataset, I was, you know, the dataset I was training

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:16.970
on, there was no M-NIST or anything like that back then.

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:19.930
So, you know, I drew a bunch of characters with my mouse.

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:23.210
I had an Amiga personal computer, which was the best computer ever.

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:27.370
And, you know, I drew a bunch of characters and then use that, uh,

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:30.970
your data augmentation to kind of, you know, increase it and then use that

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:32.530
as a way to test the performance.

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:35.650
And I compared things like, you know, fully connected nets, locally connected

00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:38.370
nets without shared weights and then shared weight networks, which was

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:39.410
basically the first comment.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:44.930
And, um, and that worked really well for small, you know, relatively small datasets,

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:48.050
could show that you get better performance and no over-training

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:49.450
with the computational architecture.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:55.770
Um, and when I got to Bell Labs in October, 1988, the first thing I did

00:08:55.770 --> 00:09:00.810
was, uh, first scale up the network because we had faster computers.

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:04.610
A few months before I got to Bell Labs, my boss at the time, Larry Jackal,

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:08.610
who became my department head at Bell Labs said, um, oh, we should order

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:09.850
a computer for you before you come.

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:10.370
What do you want?

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:13.730
They say, well, you know, here, just here in Toronto, there is a Sun 4,

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:15.210
which was the latest, greatest stuff.

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:17.370
It'd be great if we, if we had one.

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:21.410
Um, and they ordered one and I had one for myself.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:23.530
You know, at, at University of Toronto, it was one for the

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:24.410
entire department, right?

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:25.290
Yeah, it went just for me.

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:28.690
Right. And so what Larry told me is said, um, yeah, you know, at Bell

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:30.250
Labs, you don't get famous by saving money.

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:33.170
So, so that was like awesome.

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:39.250
And, and they had been working already for, for, for a while on character

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:43.650
recognition, they had this enormous dataset called USPS that had 5,000

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:44.370
training samples.

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:49.330
And so, so immediately I, you know, trained a design accomplishment net,

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:54.290
which was learn at one, basically, um, and, uh, and, and train it on this

00:09:54.290 --> 00:09:56.810
dataset and got really good results, but you know, better results than

00:09:56.810 --> 00:10:00.450
the other methods that, uh, they had been, that had been, they had tried

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:02.570
on it and that other people tried on this dataset.

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:07.370
Um, so that, uh, we knew we had something fairly early on.

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:10.450
This is, this was within three months of, of, of me joining, uh,

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:11.410
joining Bell Labs.

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:15.850
And so that was the first version of commercial net where, uh, we had, uh,

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:19.930
a competition with stride and we did not have separate sub-sampling and

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:20.650
pulling layers.

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:24.410
So each, each competition was actually sub-sampling directly.

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:28.530
And the reason for this is that we just could not afford to have a

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:30.010
convolution at every location.

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:31.690
You know, there was just too much computation.

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:41.010
Um, so the second version, um, uh, had a separate convolution and pulling

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:41.970
layer and sub-sampling.

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:46.250
Um, I guess that's the one that's called the net, the net one really.

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:49.970
Uh, we, you know, we published a couple of papers on this at, uh,

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:51.610
in neural computation and at NIPS.

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:56.530
Um, and so interesting, sorry, I gave a talk at NIPS about, about,

00:10:56.610 --> 00:11:00.650
about this work and Jeff Hinton was in the audience and then I, you

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:01.570
know, I came back to my seat.

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:04.610
I was sitting next to him and he said, you know, there's one bit of

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:07.810
information in your, in your talk, which is that if you do all the

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:09.330
sensible things, it actually works.

00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:15.410
And in fact, shortly after a deadline of work went on to make history,

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:18.370
because we came widely adopted these ideas, we can widely adopted

00:11:18.370 --> 00:11:19.690
for reading checks and.

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:20.490
Yeah.

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:25.370
So they become widely adopted within AT&T, but not, not very much outside.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:31.050
And, you know, I think it's a little difficult for me to really understand

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:34.570
why, but, um, there's several factors, I think.

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:39.690
So, you know, this was back in the late eighties and there was no internet.

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:42.890
You know, we had email with FTP, but there was no internet really.

00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:47.410
Uh, nobody, you know, no two labs were using the same software or hardware

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:48.090
platform, right?

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:50.730
You know, some people had some workstations or those at, you know, other

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:52.730
machines, you know, some people were using PCs, whatever.

00:11:52.970 --> 00:11:56.290
There was no such thing as Python or MATLAB or anything like that, right?

00:11:56.290 --> 00:11:57.530
People were writing their own code.

00:11:57.970 --> 00:12:02.290
I had written, I had spent a year and a half basically writing me and

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:06.730
Leon Batroux, uh, when he was still a student, we're working together and,

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:10.570
uh, we spent a year and a half basically just writing a neural net simulator.

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:14.490
Uh, and at the time, you know, because there was no MATLAB or Python, you

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:15.890
had to write your own interpreter, right?

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:16.810
To kind of control it.

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:18.090
So we wrote our own Lisp interpreter.

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:22.770
Uh, and so all the, the net was written in Lisp, uh, using a numeral

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:26.210
callback and, uh, very similar to what we have now with, you know, blocks

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:29.450
that you can interconnect and sort of my differentiation and all that stuff

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:33.970
that we familiar with now with, you know, torch and pytorch and TensorFlow

